{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "Business Model",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Directly answers the query's 'business models' axis, giving a categorical grouping variable with good spread across SaaS/Hardware/Media/VC etc. Most samples are well-grounded (23andMe->Consumer, 5AM Ventures->VC/Investment, Addepar->SaaS). However Accel ('we partner with founders...growth') is a venture firm yet labeled 'Data/Analytics', a clear misread, and the large 'Other' bucket suggests some imprecision. Still, values are mostly sound readings of the descriptions."
    },
    {
      "name": "Primary Customer",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.65,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Provides a distinct axis (customer type) that is not redundant with Business Model and helps answer the 'customers' half of the query. Grounding is weaker: for 5AM Ventures the text mentions 'investors,' and 'Enterprise' is a questionable specific; the dominant 'B2B/Other' catch-all is often inferred rather than stated. Consumer/Developer distinctions are reasonable where text supports them, but several values are confident specifics the terse descriptions don't clearly warrant."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
  "source_groundedness": 0.68,
  "rationale": "Both columns map tightly onto the two facets of the query (business models and customers) and provide genuine, non-redundant grouping axes for downstream analysis. Groundedness is generally acceptable—categorisations follow from the descriptions in most sampled rows—but there is observable slippage: an outright miscategorisation (Accel as Data/Analytics) and some over-confident customer labels inferred from silent or ambiguous text. Not judging completeness; on the two necessary conditions the set is useful with moderate grounding concerns."
}

